# Neo Tune parameters (docs/specs/neo-tune.md). The order changes only with a version (machines.md §3.2): songs and presets
# store values by position. Regenerate: python3 tools/gen-machine-params.py Machines/NeoTune
namespace NeoBuzz.Tune
class NeoTuneMachine
summary Pitch correction for a single voice or a melodic line. It follows the pitch of the input, moves it to the nearest note of a key and scale (or to the notes a MIDI keyboard holds) at a chosen speed, and re-synthesises the sound (by resampling and splicing whole periods for small corrections, by pitch-synchronous overlap-add when the formants or a large transpose are used), so the timbre stays the singer's. The Natural control is the main knob: 0 % is the hard tune effect, 100 % a transparent correction that moves only the centre of each settled note and leaves the vibrato; it sets the fine controls (Retune speed, Correction zone, Keep vibrato, Humanize, Transition, Note stickiness), which can be edited after. Retune speed 0 is the hard, robotic effect, a slower speed leaves the glide between notes; Humanize and Keep singer's vibrato leave the natural drift and vibrato of held notes; Amount blends the correction in. Formant preserve keeps the vowel colour when the pitch moves, Formant shift changes it for deeper or smaller voices, Transpose moves the whole voice by up to two octaves, Add vibrato puts a steady vibrato on a flat note. Range picks the tracker and the latency: Low latency (voices above 100 Hz, about 10 ms, for live use), Quality (about 28 ms) or Full (down to bass, about 36 ms); the latency is reported to NeoBuzz Live. Unvoiced sounds (breaths, s and t) and silence pass through unchanged. Use it on a lead vocal, a harmony or a solo instrument that has one note at a time.

group Key
Source     "Key from"       0 1     0     enum Set Listen | Set: the key and scale below. Listen: the key is worked out from the notes sung so far (the app has no song-wide key yet)
    how Chooses where the key comes from: the Key and Scale controls (Set), or the notes the machine has heard (Listen; the scale type stays as set).
    sound Listen lets the voice settle into the key it already sings in, which suits a first draft; Set is exact.
Key        "Key"            0 11    0     enum "C" "C#" D "D#" E F "F#" G "G#" A "A#" B | The root note of the scale (ignored for Chromatic and Custom)
    how Sets the root note of the scale, C to B.
    sound Moves the allowed notes up or down so they fit the song's key.
Scale      "Scale"          0 12    1     enum Chromatic Major Minor Dorian Phrygian Lydian Mixolydian Locrian Harmonic_minor Minor_pentatonic Major_pentatonic Blues Custom | Which notes the voice may land on; Custom uses the 12-note mask (click the keys in the editor)
    how Chooses the set of allowed notes: chromatic (all 12), the major, minor and church modes, harmonic minor, the pentatonics, blues, or a custom mask.
    sound Fewer notes pull harder and sound more obviously corrected; Chromatic only fixes the voice to the nearest semitone.
Notes      "Custom notes"   0 4095  2741  int 0 | Custom note mask: bit n is pitch class n, C = bit 0 (default C major); used when Scale is Custom
    how The twelve notes of the Custom scale as a number: C is 1, C sharp 2, D 4 and so on, added together.
    sound Allows exactly the notes you click in the editor's keyboard, in every octave.
group Pitch
Retune     "Retune speed"   0 1000  250   pow 0 400 2 ms 0                    | How fast the pitch moves to the note: 0 ms is instant (hard), 400 ms is a slow glide
    how Sets how long the voice takes to reach the target note, from 0 to 400 ms.
    sound 0 gives the hard, stepped robotic sound; 20 to 80 ms sounds natural; the longest lets the voice slide between notes.
Humanize   "Humanize"        0 1000  300   pct                                 | How much of a held note's own slow drift is kept
    how Sets how much of the singer's slow drift around a held note stays, from 0 to 100 %.
    sound At 0 a held note is dead flat; higher values keep it alive and human.
Amount     "Correction"     0 1000  1000  pct                                 | How much of the correction is applied: 0 % leaves the voice as sung, 100 % moves it fully to the note
    how Blends the correction in, from 0 to 100 % of the distance to the note (in cents).
    sound Lower values tune the voice only part of the way, which hides the effect.
Transpose  "Transpose"      0 48    24    int -24 st signed                   | Moves the whole voice up or down by whole semitones, after the correction
    how Moves the corrected voice by whole semitones, from -24 to +24.
    sound An octave up or down gives a different voice; the formant setting decides if it stays a human size.
group Formant
Preserve   "Formant preserve" 0 1   1     switch                              | On: the vowel colour stays where it was when the pitch moves. Off: the vowel colour moves with the pitch (a sped-up or slowed voice)
    how Keeps the spectral envelope (the vowel) in place while the pitch changes, or lets it follow the pitch.
    sound On sounds like the same person singing another note; off sounds like a smaller or larger singer.
Formant    "Formant shift"  0 24    12    int -12 st signed                   | Moves the vowel colour by whole semitones, up for a smaller voice, down for a larger one
    how Shifts the formants (the vowel colour) by -12 to +12 semitones, on top of Formant preserve.
    sound Up makes a younger or smaller character, down a deeper and heavier one; the extremes turn robotic.
group Vibrato
VibKeep    "Keep vibrato"   0 1000  1000  pct                                 | How much of the singer's own vibrato stays in the corrected voice
    how Passes the fast pitch movement of the singer's vibrato through the correction, from 0 to 100 %.
    sound At 0 the hard correction flattens every vibrato; at 100 % it stays as sung.
VibDepth   "Add vibrato"    0 1000  0     lin 0 100 cents 0                   | Depth of a steady vibrato added on a held note
    how Adds an even vibrato of 0 to 100 cents on top of the corrected note.
    sound Gives a plain, tuned note some life; zero adds nothing.
VibRate    "Vibrato rate"   0 1000  588   lin 0.5 9 Hz 1                     | Speed of the added vibrato
    how Sets the speed of the added vibrato from 0.5 to 9 Hz.
    sound Around 5 to 6 Hz sounds like a singer; slower is a gentle wave.
VibDelay   "Vibrato delay"  0 1000  300   lin 0 1000 ms 0                    | How long a note is held before the added vibrato fades in
    how Sets the time after a new note before the added vibrato starts, from 0 to 1000 ms.
    sound Singers start a note straight and add vibrato later; this copies that.
group Target
Target     "Target"         0 1     0     enum Scale MIDI                     | Scale: the notes of the key and scale. MIDI: the notes held on the machine's MIDI input (the key and scale are ignored)
    how Chooses what the voice is moved to: the scale's notes, or the notes held on the MIDI input.
    sound MIDI lets a keyboard or a clip conduct the voice, and with a chord held it picks the nearest chord tone.
MidiOctave "MIDI octave"    0 1     0     enum Nearest Exact                  | Nearest: a held note counts in every octave, so the voice stays in its own range. Exact: only the octave played
    how In MIDI mode, whether a held note counts in all octaves (Nearest) or only the one played (Exact).
    sound Nearest keeps the voice where it was and just changes the note; Exact makes the voice sing the played pitch.
group Engine
Range      "Range"          0 2     1     enum Low_latency Quality Full       | Low latency: voices above 100 Hz, about 10 ms. Quality: voices above 70 Hz, about 28 ms. Full: down to bass, about 36 ms. The latency is reported to the app
    how Picks the pitch tracker and the grain length, and with them the latency: 10, 28 or 36 ms.
    sound Low latency is for playing live and a little less steady on low voices; Quality is the best sound; Full also follows bass voices and instruments.
Input      "Input"          0 1     0     enum Sum Left                       | Which channel the voice is taken from: the sum of left and right, or the left channel only
    how Chooses the input: the two channels added, or the left channel only.
    sound The corrected voice is always mono; the dry sound stays in stereo.
Transients "Transients"     0 1     1     switch                              | Keeps the sharp start of a sound (a b, d or p) as sung instead of re-synthesising it
    how Lets the first few milliseconds of a hard consonant or a plucked note pass through unprocessed.
    sound On keeps consonants crisp; off treats them like the rest of the voice.
group Output
Mix        "Mix"            0 1000  1000  pct                                 | Wet against dry; at 0 % the output is the delayed input, bit for bit
    how Blends the corrected voice with the original (delayed to match).
    sound Parallel use keeps the character of the singer while the tuning shows through.

group Natural
Natural    "Natural"        0 1000  600   pct                                 | How natural the correction sounds: 0 % is the hard, robotic effect, 100 % is a transparent correction that keeps the singer's expression. Moving it sets Retune speed, Correction zone, Keep vibrato, Humanize, Transition and Note stickiness together
    how Sets how natural the correction sounds, from the hard effect (0 %) to a transparent one (100 %). In the editor it rewrites the fine controls below it in one step; they can then be edited by hand.
    sound 0 % snaps every note flat and instant; about 40 % is a pop polish; about 75 % corrects the centre of each note and leaves the vibrato, scoops and glides; 100 % is as transparent as it gets.
Zone       "Correction zone" 0 1000 760   lin 0 100 cents 0                   | How far from a note the correction still pulls: movement outside the zone passes through as sung (100 cents and more: no zone, every pitch is corrected)
    how Sets the zone around each note, from 0 to 100 cents, within which the correction applies; fades out beyond it.
    sound A narrow zone lets scoops, slides and expressive bends through and corrects only the pitch that settles near a note; 100 corrects everything.
Glide      "Transition"     0 1000  600   pct                                 | Between notes: 0 % snaps at the midpoint, 100 % leaves the singer's own glide untouched
    how Sets how note changes are handled, from a snap (0 %) to the singer's own glide kept (100 %): the correction fades while the pitch of the note centre is moving fast.
    sound Snap sounds like the effect; glide keeps slides between notes as sung.
Stick      "Note stickiness" 0 1000 600  pct                                 | How hard it is to leave a note: how far past the midpoint, and for how long, the pitch has to be before the target changes
    how Sets the hysteresis (in cents past the midpoint) and the minimum time before the target note changes, from low (0 %) to high (100 %).
    sound High stickiness stops a wide vibrato or a wobble from flipping between neighbouring notes; low follows quick note changes.
group Engine
Method     "Method"         0 2     0     enum Auto Splice PSOLA              | Auto: period splicing for small corrections, PSOLA when formants or a large transpose are used. Splice or PSOLA: always that engine
    how Chooses how the sound is re-synthesised: Auto, the splice engine (resample and repeat or drop whole periods, the most transparent) or PSOLA (grains, with formant control).
    sound Splice keeps the singer's voice almost untouched for corrections under about 2 semitones; PSOLA can move formants and transpose widely.

# What the tuner measures (NeoTuneMachine.Readings, machine_readings).
reading input_hz        Hz      | The pitch of the voice now, Hz (0 when not voiced)
reading target_note     note    | The note the voice is moved to, as a MIDI number (0 when not voiced)
reading correction_cents cents  | How far the correction moves the pitch now, cents (negative: down)
reading voiced          flag    | 1 while a pitch is found, 0 for unvoiced sounds and silence
reading latency_samples samples | The delay of the machine, reported to NeoBuzz Live
group Output
FxAmount    "Amount"        0 2000 1000 lin 0 200 % 1                  | Effect strength: 100 % = the settings as shown, 0 % = bypass (the input, delayed by the latency), 200 % = twice as far from neutral
    how Scales the correction: its strength (Correction, up to full) and the retune time. 0 % is bypass, 100 % your settings, 200 % twice as far from neutral.
    sound Turn it down to let the natural pitch through, up to correct faster and harder.
